; SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
;
; tscaly — a Scaly port of the TypeScript compiler.
;
; Ported from microsoft/typescript-go, pinned at commit 2bd066d8 (tag
; typescript/v7.0.2). See ../README.md for provenance and ../CLAUDE.md for the
; working rules — in particular the Go→Scaly mapping vocabulary, which must be
; read before porting a file, and the license boundary (nothing moves from here
; into the MIT parts of this repository).
;
; Memory doctrine, decided at kickoff and to be proven by this port: a compiler
; run is a region hierarchy. The cycles the reference carries — Node.Parent and
; Type.checker pointing back at their owner — cost nothing here, where a cycle
; inside one region is freed with the region. The reference's own memo caches
; already hang off the Checker rather than off globals, so N checkers are N
; regions.
;
; Dispatch doctrine: no dynamic dispatch in the core. The reference's tag-struct
; + interface-payload shape (nodeData, TypeData) becomes a sum type plus pattern
; matching, because a trait object makes the callee unknown at compile time and
; every purity, escape and parallelism question stops there. See CLAUDE.md §3.1.

package scaly 0.1.0

define tscaly
{
    ; The syntax-kind tag. GENERATED — edit tools/genkind.py, not the file.
    ;
    ; It is declared FIRST because module declaration order decides whether a
    ; module-level constant is visible: a `define` read from a module declared
    ; LATER is a hard `unknown identifier` in operator position and, worse, is
    ; SILENTLY DROPPED along with the whole call when it appears as a call
    ; argument. Every module below reads these constants.
    module Kind

    ; Per-token scanner flags. Hand-ported; short and readable.
    module TokenFlags

    ; Per-node parser/binder flags. Hand-ported, like TokenFlags, and declared
    ; here for the same reason Kind is: the parser reads these constants.
    module NodeFlags

    ; Per-modifier-keyword flags. Hand-ported like TokenFlags and NodeFlags, and
    ; declared up here for the same reason: the parser reads these constants, and
    ; a `define` read from a module declared LATER is silently dropped in
    ; call-argument position.
    module ModifierFlags

    ; Per-symbol flags and the EXCLUDES sets the binder tests a declaration
    ; against. GENERATED — edit tools/gensymbolflags.py, not the file; the
    ; generator's header has the argument for why this table is generated while
    ; NodeFlags and ModifierFlags are hand-ported. Declared up here with the other
    ; constant tables, for the same reason they are.
    module SymbolFlags

    ; The checker's TYPE flags, and the composites the checker tests a type's
    ; flags against. GENERATED — edit tools/gentypeflags.py, not the file; its
    ; header has the argument for why this table is generated (the composites, and
    ; the fact that the bit POSITIONS are an ORDER a union's printed form depends
    ; on). Declared up here with the other constant tables for the same reason
    ; they are.
    module TypeFlags

    ; ast.IsDeclarationNode, as a question about the KIND. GENERATED — edit
    ; tools/gendeclarationkinds.py, not the file; its header has the argument for
    ; why this one CANNOT be hand-written (the reference asks it of the node
    ; LAYOUT, not of the kind, and seven of the fifty-six answers are ones no
    ; reader would guess). Declared up here with the other constant tables,
    ; because it reads the Kind constants and every module below reads it.
    module DeclarationKinds

    ; The TS error numbers. GENERATED — edit tools/gendiagnostics.py, not the
    ; file. Declared here for the same reason Kind is: both the scanner and the
    ; parser read these constants, and a `define` read from a module declared
    ; LATER is silently dropped in call-argument position.
    module DiagnosticCodes

    ; The parser's diagnostic LIST — shared with the scanner, which is what the
    ; reference's ErrorCallback reduces to here. Declared before the scanner,
    ; which appends to it.
    module Diagnostics

    ; The keyword table + GetIdentifierToken. GENERATED — edit
    ; tools/genkeywords.py, not the file.
    module Keywords

    ; The ECMAScript identifier code-point tables. GENERATED — edit
    ; tools/genunicode.py, not the file. Declared before the scanner, which
    ; calls into it.
    module UnicodeId

    ; The ECMAScript Number conversions — internal/jsnum, plus the decimal↔binary
    ; machinery of Go's own strconv that it delegates to. Declared before the
    ; scanner, which normalizes every numeric literal's value through it; it reads
    ; nothing from any other module.
    module jsnum

    ; The scanner. Everything reachable from Scan() is ported; what remains is
    ; parser-driven (the re-scan entry points, JSX, comment directives) and is
    ; listed at `unported_pos`.
    module scanner

    ; The syntax tree. The reference's tag-struct-plus-interface becomes a
    ; tag-struct plus a SUM TYPE — see CLAUDE.md §3.1 and the file header.
    module ast

    ; The one function of internal/tspath the parser asks for: does this file's
    ; NAME make it a declaration file. Declared before the parser, which calls
    ; it; it reads nothing from any other module.
    module tspath

    ; The parser. Slice 1: the source-file spine, the statements a file of
    ; plain declarations is made of, and primary expressions. Everything else
    ; reports its own position (`unported_pos`) rather than building a tree
    ; that disagrees with the reference while looking well-formed.
    module parser

    ; The BINDER: symbols, symbol tables and the bind diagnostics. Declared after
    ; the parser because it reads nothing the parser does not already declare and
    ; because it calls into it for one question (is this file an external module).
    ; Slice 27 ports the skeleton and the variable/function/parameter arms, slice 28
    ; the MODULE SYMBOL (an external module's own symbol, the JSON arm's second one,
    ; and the two symbols an exported declaration owns), slice 29 the import/export
    ; ALIAS family (the four kinds that share one arm, the import clause, the export
    ; declaration, the export assignment and the UMD global); everything else in
    ; either switch reports itself as unported.
    module binder

    ; jsnum's own instrument (slice 44), and the same argument PathCheck makes one
    ; paragraph down: the corpus sees a numeric literal's VALUE in 27 units, and
    ; every other arm of the conversion is unexercised and therefore unmeasured.
    ; tests/numcheck.sh diffs this against the reference's own jsnum.
    module NumCheck

    ; The absolute-path arms' own instrument (slice 28): the same generated corpus
    ; goes through this and through the reference's tspath, and tests/pathcheck.sh
    ; diffs the two. Not a port of anything either — the corpus cannot reach the
    ; root scanner's UNC/DOS/URL arms or the segment loop's `..`, and an
    ; unexercised arm is indistinguishable from a right one.
    module PathCheck

    ; OUR half of the binder yardstick's dump — not a port of anything; the format
    ; is ours and tests/oracle/symbols.go is its other producer. It lives in the
    ; package rather than in the dumper program because the numbering needs
    ; containers, and a generic instance handed to a program root reads back as
    ; garbage (root CLAUDE.md).
    module SymbolDump

    ; The CHECKER: the semantic diagnostics of a file and the type of a node.
    ; Declared after the binder because it asks it two questions (is this file a
    ; module, and what is a module declaration's instance state) and because it
    ; reads nothing the binder does not already declare. Slice 47 ports the
    ; SKELETON — the type representation, the intrinsic types, the printer's
    ; keyword arms, the file-level check and getTypeOfNode's chain — with a REPORT
    ; at every arm that is not ported, which is what makes the unported column the
    ; work list for the slices after it.
    module checker

    ; OUR half of the checker yardstick's dump — not a port of anything; the format
    ; is ours and tests/oracle/types.go is its other producer. In the package
    ; rather than in the dumper program for SymbolDump's reason, and after `checker`
    ; because it reads its types.
    module TypeDump
}
